#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151832 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152073 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "398716c2-d0e6-5106-a702-2f71d957726f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Integers"}] :question/id #uuid "e64a4c4d-502b-508e-b7af-b4012cd16617" :question/math-academy-id "e-14578" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $24 \\equiv 12 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,3)$\n2. $17 \\equiv 21 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$\n3. $19 \\equiv 12 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,9)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid (a-b)\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a true statement. Indeed, $3 \\mid (24-12)$. There is no remainder:\n$(24-12) \\div 3 = 12 \\div 3 = 4$\n- Case II gives a true statement. Indeed, $4 \\mid (17-21)$. There is no remainder:\n$(17-21) \\div 4 = -4 \\div 4 = -1$\n- Case III gives a false statement because $9 \\not\\mid (19-12)$. Notice that $19-12 = 7$, which is not divisible by $9$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151833 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152074 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2eebe007-8a95-578d-b553-e37a7bd8a327" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Negative Integers"}] :question/id #uuid "041ab8f3-ced5-5a79-b639-190850c06891" :question/math-academy-id "e-14579" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $-11 \\equiv -29 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,6)$\n2. $90 \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,9)$\n3. $-10 \\equiv 4 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,5)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid (a-b)\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true because $6 \\mid (-11-(-29))$. There is no remainder:\n$(-11-(-29)) \\div 6 = 18\\div 6 = 3$\n- Statement II is true because $9 \\, \\mid \\, (90-0)$. There is no remainder:\n$(90-0) \\div 9 = 90\\div 9 = 10$\n- Statement III is false because $5 \\not\\mid (-10-4)$. Notice that $-10-4 = -14$, which is not divisible by $5$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151835 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152075 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07c5ec7-8199-53c7-9738-80e360d03b38" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Modulus"}] :question/id #uuid "afcb17e2-d38b-5ebc-b135-f8cf68a601ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-14581" :question/problem "Given that $-7 \\equiv 17 \\,(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$, which of the following could be the value of $n$?\n\n1. $n=6$\n2. $n=5$\n3. $n=4$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid (a-b)\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have\n\n$$\na-b = -7-17= -24\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $6 \\, \\mid \\, (-24)$ because $-24 \\div 6 = -4$, and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=6$.\n- Note that $5 \\!\\not{\\mid} \\, (-24)$. Therefore, the statement is false when $n=5$.\n- Note that $4 \\, \\mid \\, (-24)$ because $-24 \\div 4 = -6$, and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=4$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152747 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152074 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2eebe007-8a95-578d-b553-e37a7bd8a327" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Negative Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adb500bf-0267-5484-81be-fa6afb5ab797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150990} {:answer/id #uuid "e574c944-1af9-5ba0-8f5c-b88a3366de99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150991} {:answer/id #uuid "f3615113-ab5d-511f-a6d8-9a31f60cddb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150992} {:answer/id #uuid "35da42a6-5910-5088-8a6c-8137e3ffd88c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150993} {:answer/id #uuid "7ddd6e47-a9fe-5a3c-8642-02d8067596aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150994}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f92f5252-6905-5d03-9e4c-9f984e8614ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150989}] :question/id #uuid "bc725849-ec72-5565-a2a3-c18e7bade536" :question/math-academy-id "q-76784" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $11≡1(mod5)$\n2. $27≡15(mod7)$\n3. $30≡ - 18(mod4)$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152073 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "398716c2-d0e6-5106-a702-2f71d957726f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9edaa9f5-61ee-57f6-bbeb-15fd7c42fd32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151056} {:answer/id #uuid "b0fabceb-1119-5eaf-8918-e6863227c024" :answer/type {:db/